export const booksL = [
  u({ slug: "harry-potter-and-the-order-of-the-phoenix", title: "Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix", author: "J.K. Rowling", year: 2003, series: { name: "Harry Potter", slug: "harry-potter", index: 5 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Scholastic (US)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "Harry at fifteen: angry, shut out and right. I stamp it twelve-plus, like Goblet of Fire: the romance is one damp kiss, but a teacher makes Harry carve words into his own hand, and the death at the end is the one that hurts readers most.",
    summary: "The summer after Voldemort's return, nobody believes Harry. The Ministry of Magic calls him a liar, the Daily Prophet mocks him, and when two Dementors attack him and his cousin Dudley in a Surrey alley, Harry is put on trial for using magic to save them. He spends the rest of the holiday at 12 Grimmauld Place, headquarters of the Order of the Phoenix, with his godfather Sirius. At Hogwarts the Ministry installs Dolores Umbridge, a smiling official in pink who refuses to teach practical defence and punishes Harry with a quill that writes in his own blood. Hermione's answer is Dumbledore's Army, a secret club where Harry teaches his classmates to fight. Harry kisses Cho Chang, dreams of a locked door and sees Mr Weasley attacked by a snake. The dreams are a trap, and they lead six students into the Department of Mysteries.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up from me. Many ten- and eleven-year-olds read it straight after Goblet of Fire, and a confident reader can, but two things are worth knowing. The first is Umbridge. Her detentions, in which Harry writes 'I must not tell lies' with a quill that cuts the words into the back of his hand, are the most upsetting image of cruelty in the series because the cruelty comes from an adult in charge of children. The second is Sirius. He is killed by his cousin Bellatrix in the final battle and falls through a veiled archway; there is no body, and Harry's grief, and his rage at Dumbledore afterwards, run for pages. Romance is Harry's crush on Cho, a kiss under the mistletoe that leaves her crying, and Hermione explaining girls to the boys. Language stays at 'bloody' and 'git'. HBO's series begins with book one on December 25, 2026; this is the book where families who are reading along tend to pause.",
    publisherNote: "Scholastic in the US and Bloomsbury in the UK publish the whole series as middle grade. The 2007 film was rated PG-13. I place the book where I placed Goblet of Fire.",
    seriesNote: "Book five of seven, and the longest. Half-Blood Prince adds snogging, a curse that leaves a boy bleeding on a bathroom floor and the death of the series' great protector; I keep it at twelve-plus. Deathly Hallows, a war book with torture and a long list of deaths, is the one I move to fourteen-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's MPA rating as context and long-standing consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Harry Potter: Wizarding World", url: "https://www.wizardingworld.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A crush on Cho Chang and one tearful kiss under the mistletoe."], [3, "A Dementor attack, a snake mauling, a battle with Death Eaters and the death of a godfather."], [1, "'Bloody' and 'git'."], [1, "Butterbeer, and a house-elf who drinks to cope."], [2, "An abusive teacher, grief, isolation and a government that lies."]] }),

  u({ slug: "harry-potter-and-the-half-blood-prince", title: "Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince", author: "J.K. Rowling", year: 2005, series: { name: "Harry Potter", slug: "harry-potter", index: 6 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Scholastic (US)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "Memories of Tom Riddle, a potions book with notes in the margin, and a lot of snogging. I keep it at twelve-plus: the romance is the series' most, but still kisses, and the violence is sharp but short, until the death at the top of the Astronomy Tower.",
    summary: "In Harry's sixth year, Dumbledore begins private lessons that are really trips into other people's memories, showing Harry how an orphan called Tom Riddle became Lord Voldemort and how he split his soul to cheat death. Harry finds an old potions textbook annotated by someone who calls himself the Half-Blood Prince and becomes the best student in the class overnight. Draco Malfoy has been given a task by Voldemort and is falling apart trying to carry it out. Ron dates Lavender Brown, Hermione is furious, and Harry realises he is in love with Ginny. A cursed necklace and a glass of poisoned mead nearly kill two students. Dumbledore takes Harry to a sea cave to find one of the pieces of Voldemort's soul, and they come back to a castle full of Death Eaters.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up. The romance is the comedy of the book: Ron and Lavender kissing in every corridor, Ron eating love-potion chocolates by mistake, and Harry and Ginny's first kiss in front of the whole common room. Nothing goes further. The violence is concentrated in a few scenes. Harry uses a spell from the margins of his book, Sectumsempra, on Draco in a bathroom, and Draco is slashed and bleeding on the flooded floor until Snape arrives. In the cave, Dumbledore drinks a potion that makes him beg for it to stop, and the dead rise from the water. The werewolf Fenrir Greyback, who says he likes to bite children, savages Bill Weasley's face. Then Snape kills Dumbledore. Slughorn drinks heavily, and he and Hagrid get drunk at a funeral for a giant spider. The heaviest note is Draco: a frightened boy ordered to murder or see his family killed.",
    publisherNote: "Scholastic in the US, middle grade. The 2009 film was rated PG. The book is darker than the film in the cave and the bathroom.",
    seriesNote: "Book six of seven. Deathly Hallows is the one I move up a band, to fourteen-plus, for torture, the war and the number of deaths.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's MPA rating as context and long-standing consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Harry Potter: Wizarding World", url: "https://www.wizardingworld.com/" }],
    sig: [[2, "Lots of kissing, a love potion played for laughs and the first real couples."], [3, "A slashing curse, poisonings, a werewolf attack and the murder of a headmaster."], [1, "Mild."], [2, "Slughorn drinks heavily; two adults get drunk at a funeral; poisoned mead."], [2, "A boy forced to plan a murder, grief, an orphan's path to evil."]] }),

  u({ slug: "harry-potter-and-the-deathly-hallows", title: "Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows", author: "J.K. Rowling", year: 2007, series: { name: "Harry Potter", slug: "harry-potter", index: 7 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Scholastic (US)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The war book. I stamp it fourteen-plus, a band above the rest of the series: there is no sex and almost no swearing, but a woman is killed and fed to a snake in the opening pages, a teenager is tortured on a drawing-room floor, and the final battle kills characters readers have loved for six books.",
    summary: "Harry, Ron and Hermione do not go back to Hogwarts. Dumbledore has left them a job: find and destroy the remaining Horcruxes, the pieces of Voldemort's soul. The Ministry falls in the first chapters, Muggle-born witches and wizards are rounded up, and the three of them go on the run, camping in a tent through a long winter with a locket that poisons whoever wears it. Ron leaves. On Christmas Eve, Harry and Hermione visit his parents' grave in Godric's Hollow and are ambushed. They learn about the Deathly Hallows, three legendary objects that together are said to master death. Captured and brought to Malfoy Manor, they escape at a terrible cost. The book ends in the Battle of Hogwarts, Snape's memories, and a walk into the Forbidden Forest that Harry does not expect to come back from.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up from me, with the honest note that this is the book thousands of twelve-year-olds race to after Half-Blood Prince, and most cope. Here is what a parent is weighing. Violence: a captured teacher is killed at the Malfoys' table and fed to Nagini; Bellatrix tortures Hermione with the Cruciatus Curse and holds a knife to her throat; Dobby is killed by a thrown knife; Snape's throat is torn by the snake; and the battle kills Fred, Lupin, Tonks and Colin Creevey, too young to fight, who came back anyway. The scene in Godric's Hollow, where a snake bursts out of an old woman's corpse, is the series' one real horror moment. Romance is kisses, and a vision from the locket of Harry and Hermione together that is meant to torment Ron. Molly Weasley's one swear word, at Bellatrix, is the line everyone remembers. The ending is hopeful. HBO's adaptation starts with book one on December 25, 2026, which gives younger readers years before they reach this one.",
    publisherNote: "Scholastic in the US, middle grade, like the rest of the series. Both films were rated PG-13. I break from the publisher here because the content in the last third is war fiction.",
    seriesNote: "Book seven of seven. The play Harry Potter and the Cursed Child, set nineteen years later, is gentler and sits around twelve-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the films' MPA ratings as context and long-standing consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Harry Potter: Wizarding World", url: "https://www.wizardingworld.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "Kisses, and a jealous vision conjured by the locket."], [4, "Torture, a body fed to a snake, a battle with many named deaths."], [2, "Mostly mild; one memorable swear word at the climax."], [1, "Firewhisky at a wedding and a wake."], [3, "War, persecution of a minority, the deaths of friends and parents, a hero walking to his own death."]] }),

  u({ slug: "percy-jackson-the-last-olympian", title: "The Last Olympian", author: "Rick Riordan", year: 2009, series: { name: "Percy Jackson and the Olympians", slug: "percy-jackson-and-the-olympians", index: 5 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Disney-Hyperion", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The battle for Manhattan, and the end of Percy's first series. I keep it at ten-plus: it has the series' highest death count and its first real kiss, but the jokes hold, nothing is gory, and the ending is earned.",
    summary: "Percy turns sixteen, and the prophecy about a child of the eldest gods reaching that age is due. Kronos, the Titan lord, now living in Luke's body, marches on Olympus, which sits on the six-hundredth floor of the Empire State Building, while the gods are away fighting the storm giant Typhon across America. Percy bathes in the River Styx to become invulnerable, with one weak spot only he knows. Morpheus puts New York to sleep, and the demigods of Camp Half-Blood defend the bridges and tunnels of Manhattan against an army of monsters. A spy is discovered inside the camp. Annabeth is wounded protecting Percy. The last fight is in the throne room of the gods, and the choice that ends it belongs not to Percy but to Luke.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up. It is a war book in middle-grade clothes: the opening chapter kills Beckendorf, a camper readers like, and named demigods keep dying through the battle, including a girl who turns out to have been the spy and dies trying to make up for it. Luke's death at the end is quiet and sad. Nothing is described in blood. Percy and Annabeth finally kiss, and their friends throw them in the lake for it. The heavier idea, and the one worth talking about, is why Luke turned: the gods ignored their children, and Percy's reward for winning is to ask them to stop. Disney+'s season 3, adapting The Titan's Curse, premieres November 20, 2026, and this is the book a reader who reaches the end of the show will want next.",
    publisherNote: "Disney-Hyperion, middle grade. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "Book five of five. The Heroes of Olympus series continues at the same band, with more romance but no more heat. The 2023 Chalice of the Gods and 2024 Wrath of the Triple Goddess are lighter side stories.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent parent and reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Rick Riordan: Percy Jackson series", url: "https://rickriordan.com/series/percy-jackson/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A first real kiss, and a dunking in the lake for it."], [2, "A city-wide battle with named deaths; bloodless."], [0, "Clean."], [0, "None."], [2, "The deaths of friends and of an enemy who was once a friend; neglectful parents."]] }),

  u({ slug: "diary-of-a-wimpy-kid", title: "Diary of a Wimpy Kid", author: "Jeff Kinney", year: 2007, series: { name: "Diary of a Wimpy Kid", slug: "diary-of-a-wimpy-kid", index: 1 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Amulet Books (Abrams)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "Greg Heffley's journal, which he insists is not a diary. I stamp it ten-plus by this site's floor: there is nothing here a seven-year-old cannot handle, and the only thing to discuss is that Greg is often a bad friend and the book knows it.",
    summary: "Greg Heffley is starting middle school, which he describes as the dumbest idea ever invented, and his mother has bought him a journal. He fills it with plans to become popular, complaints about his older brother Rodrick and his little brother Manny, and the misadventures of his best friend Rowley, who is cheerfully uncool. There is the Cheese Touch, a piece of mouldy cheese on the basketball court that nobody dares touch; a Halloween where teenagers chase the boys with a garden hose; a school safety patrol job that goes wrong when Greg takes kindergartners the long way home and lets Rowley take the blame; and Rowley's comic strip, Zoo-Wee Mama!, which becomes more popular than anything Greg makes. The book is half words, half stick-figure drawings, and the joke on every page is that Greg believes he is the hero.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up by this site's scale, and fine from about seven, which is where most fans start. Searches for 'is Diary of a Wimpy Kid appropriate for a seven-year-old' are really asking whether the humour is mean, and the honest answer is that it is sometimes. Greg lies, takes credit, and treats Rowley badly, and the book lets the reader see that without lecturing; at the end, Greg does one genuinely decent thing for his friend. Violence is slapstick. Language is name-calling, 'moron' and 'jerk'. There is no romance, no swearing and no substances. A child who reads it at seven will laugh at Greg; a child who reads it at eleven will recognise him. The 2026 news for fans is book 21, Fight or Flight, out in October, and a new Disney+ Christmas film, The Getaway, on December 4.",
    publisherNote: "Amulet Books, an imprint of Abrams, publishes the series for ages eight to twelve. Content matches the lower end of that range.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of twenty-one. The series stays in the same band throughout; later books add Greg's crushes and a few more gross-out jokes, nothing more. The Getaway, book twelve, is the Christmas one.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and long-standing consistent parent and teacher reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Wimpy Kid: official site", url: "https://wimpykid.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None."], [1, "Slapstick: a garden hose, a wrestling unit, a scuffle."], [1, "Name-calling: 'moron', 'jerk'."], [0, "None."], [1, "Selfishness, a friendship tested, middle-school social pressure."]] }),

  u({ slug: "diary-of-a-wimpy-kid-the-getaway", title: "Diary of a Wimpy Kid: The Getaway", author: "Jeff Kinney", year: 2017, series: { name: "Diary of a Wimpy Kid", slug: "diary-of-a-wimpy-kid", index: 12 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Amulet Books (Abrams)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The Heffleys skip Christmas at home for a tropical resort, and everything goes wrong. I stamp it ten-plus by this site's floor: it is the gentlest kind of holiday disaster comedy, and the Disney+ film, out December 4, 2026, is why it is the Wimpy Kid book everyone is searching for this year.",
    summary: "The Heffleys are tired of the stress of Christmas at home: the shopping, the relatives, the decorations. So this year they trade it all for a holiday at a tropical island resort. Greg is delighted until the trip begins. The airport is a nightmare, the flight is worse, and the resort turns out to be less a paradise than a place full of things Greg would rather avoid: strange bugs and creatures, sunburn, crowded pools, food that disagrees with him and a family that cannot agree on how to relax. Greg's plans to spend the week on the water slide keep falling apart, and as the disasters pile up, the Heffleys start to wonder whether the stress they ran away from was so bad after all.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up by this site's scale, and an easy yes for a seven- or eight-year-old. Nothing in it needs a warning: the comedy is travel mishaps, gross-out moments about germs, bugs and upset stomachs, and Greg's usual self-pity. There is no romance, no swearing and no violence beyond slapstick. The resort has a bar, which the adults are seen near. The gentle message, that a family Christmas at home is worth its stress, makes it a good one to give in December. Disney+ premieres the animated film, Diary of a Wimpy Kid Christmas: The Getaway, on December 4, 2026; reading the book first is a good way to make a child feel ahead of the film.",
    publisherNote: "Amulet Books (Abrams), ages eight to twelve. A special edition with the Disney+ film's cover is due in December 2026.",
    seriesNote: "Book twelve of twenty-one. The whole series sits in this band. Cabin Fever, book six, is the other winter book, with the family snowed in before Christmas.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's release announcement and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Wimpy Kid: official site", url: "https://wimpykid.com/" }, { label: "Disney+: Diary of a Wimpy Kid Christmas: The Getaway", url: "https://www.disneyplus.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None."], [1, "Slapstick and travel mishaps."], [0, "Clean."], [1, "A resort bar, in the background."], [0, "Light: family friction on holiday."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-best-christmas-pageant-ever", title: "The Best Christmas Pageant Ever", author: "Barbara Robinson", year: 1972, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "HarperCollins", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The worst kids in town take over the church nativity play. I stamp it ten-plus by this site's floor: it is one of the gentlest books on the site, with one funny scene of children smoking cigars and a lot of bullying played for laughs, and it is the Christmas chapter book I would hand over first.",
    summary: "The six Herdman children, Ralph, Imogene, Leroy, Claude, Ollie and Gladys, are, in the narrator's words, absolutely the worst kids in the history of the world. They lie, steal, smoke cigars, hit other children and once burned down a toolshed. They have never been to church. When they hear there are snacks at Sunday school, they show up, and through a mixture of threats and confusion they end up cast as every lead in the Christmas pageant: Mary, Joseph, the three Wise Men and the Angel of the Lord. The narrator's mother, stuck directing the pageant, has to teach them the Christmas story from scratch. The Herdmans have never heard it, and their questions about Herod, the inn and the manger turn out to be better than anyone else's.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up by this site's scale, and a read-aloud for the whole family from about seven. The book is short, a single evening for a confident reader. The things that could raise an eyebrow are played as comedy: the Herdmans smoke cigars in the church bathroom until the fire department turns up, Gladys hits people, and Imogene threatens anyone who gets in her way. The reason they behave like this is told lightly: their father left, and their mother works double shifts. The ending, when Imogene, playing Mary, cries during the pageant, is the moment that makes adults tear up too. There is no romance and no swearing. The 2024 film, rated PG, is faithful.",
    publisherNote: "HarperCollins publishes it as middle grade, ages eight to twelve. Content sits at the bottom of that range.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone. Two later books feature the Herdmans, The Best School Year Ever and The Best Halloween Ever, in the same band.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, long-standing teacher and librarian consensus, the film's MPA rating as context and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "HarperCollins: The Best Christmas Pageant Ever", url: "https://www.harpercollins.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None."], [1, "Bullying and threats, played for laughs."], [0, "Clean."], [2, "Children smoke cigars in the church bathroom; played as comedy."], [1, "Neglect, poverty, an absent father."]] }),

  u({ slug: "little-women", title: "Little Women", author: "Louisa May Alcott", year: 1868, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Public domain (many editions)", publisherAge: "All ages",
    verdict: "Four sisters, one Christmas without presents and a lifetime after it. I stamp it ten-plus: there is nothing in it to shield a child from, but Beth's death in the second half is one of the saddest scenes in children's literature, and a ten-year-old should be ready for it.",
    summary: "'Christmas won't be Christmas without any presents,' Jo grumbles on the first page. Their father is away as a chaplain in the Civil War, money is short, and the four March sisters, responsible Meg, fiery Jo, shy Beth and vain Amy, give their Christmas breakfast to a poor family down the road. The first half follows a year in their lives: the rich boy next door, Laurie, becomes Jo's best friend; Amy burns Jo's manuscript and nearly drowns falling through the ice; Meg goes to a fashionable party and is ashamed of herself; Beth catches scarlet fever. The second half, originally published as Good Wives, follows them into adulthood: Meg's marriage, Jo's refusal of Laurie and her writing life in New York, Amy's travels in Europe, and Beth.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up, and a classic gift for a ten- or eleven-year-old who likes long books. Read-aloud from eight or nine works for the first half. There is no violence beyond Amy's fall through the ice, and no swearing. Romance is proposals and engagements, conducted with great decorum. At a party, Meg drinks champagne and flirts, and regrets it. The thing to prepare a child for is grief: Beth's long decline and death are told slowly and tenderly, and many readers are blindsided by them. The other conversation is Jo's: why does she say no to Laurie? Unabridged editions are long and the language is Victorian; abridgements for younger readers cut the second half heavily. The 2019 film by Greta Gerwig is rated PG.",
    publisherNote: "Public domain, published in countless editions, from full-text classics to illustrated abridgements. I rate the full two-part text.",
    seriesNote: "Followed by Little Men and Jo's Boys, about Jo's school, in the same band.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the original text's long publication record, teacher and librarian consensus and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Project Gutenberg: Little Women", url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/514" }],
    sig: [[1, "Proposals, a refusal and courtships, all chaste."], [1, "A fall through the ice."], [0, "Clean."], [1, "Champagne at a party, and a lesson about it."], [2, "A sister's death, a father at war, poverty."]] }),

  u({ slug: "wonder", title: "Wonder", author: "R.J. Palacio", year: 2012, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Knopf Books for Young Readers", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "A boy with a facial difference starts school for the first time at ten. I stamp it ten-plus: the bullying is real, there is one fight in the woods and a family dog dies, but the book is warm, funny and short, and it starts conversations parents want to have.",
    summary: "August Pullman was born with a genetic condition that affected the shape of his face, and after twenty-seven surgeries he has been homeschooled all his life. At ten, his parents send him to fifth grade at Beecher Prep. The first part, in Auggie's voice, covers the stares, the kids who will not sit near him, and the two friends he makes: Summer, who sits with him at lunch, and Jack Will, whom Auggie overhears saying something unforgivable at Halloween. Then the book hands the story to others: his older sister Via, who loves him and is tired of being invisible; Jack; Via's boyfriend Justin; and her former best friend Miranda. A bully called Julian runs a campaign against Auggie, and on a school nature retreat a group of older kids from another school corner Auggie and Jack in the woods.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up, and widely taught in fourth and fifth grade, which is fine for most nine-year-olds. The thing parents ask about is Jack's remark: when Auggie overhears Jack telling Julian that if he looked like Auggie he would kill himself, Auggie is crushed; the book handles the remark and the apology carefully, but it is a moment to talk about. The bullying includes a game called 'the Plague', notes in Auggie's locker and kids calling him names. In the woods, older boys shove Auggie and Jack and call Auggie a freak until his classmates defend him; no one is badly hurt. The family dog, Daisy, is put down, and the scene is sad. Via and Justin's romance is a kiss. Language is mild. The 2017 film is rated PG.",
    publisherNote: "Knopf Books for Young Readers, middle grade, ages eight to twelve. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone. Auggie & Me collects three companion stories, including Julian's; White Bird, a graphic novel about Julian's grandmother in Nazi-occupied France, is heavier and sits at twelve-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's MPA rating as context and consistent teacher and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Wonder: official site", url: "https://wonderthebook.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A teenage sister's boyfriend and a kiss."], [1, "A shoving match in the woods; no serious injury."], [1, "Mild name-calling."], [0, "None."], [2, "Bullying, disability, a remark about suicide, a pet's death."]] }),

  u({ slug: "holes", title: "Holes", author: "Louis Sachar", year: 1998, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Farrar, Straus and Giroux", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "A boy is sent to a desert camp to dig holes for a crime he did not commit. I stamp it ten-plus: funny, clever and satisfying, but the backstory includes a man shot for kissing a white woman, and a parent should know that before handing it to an eight-year-old.",
    summary: "Stanley Yelnats is overweight, unlucky and blamed for everything, which his family attributes to a curse on his no-good-dirty-rotten-pig-stealing great-great-grandfather. When a pair of famous sneakers falls on his head, Stanley is convicted of stealing them and sent to Camp Green Lake, a juvenile detention camp in a dried-up lake in Texas, where every boy digs one five-foot hole a day in the baking sun. The Warden says it builds character. She is actually looking for something. Stanley teaches a silent boy called Zero to read, and when Zero runs away into the desert, Stanley follows him. The book braids his story with two others: his great-great-grandfather in Latvia, and Kissin' Kate Barlow, the schoolteacher who became an outlaw a hundred years before.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up, and a Newbery winner taught from third grade upward. Most of the book is perfectly fine for an eight-year-old, and plenty read it then. The part to know about is the Kate Barlow story. Kate, a white schoolteacher, kisses Sam, a Black onion seller, a mob burns the schoolhouse, and Sam is shot dead in his boat as he tries to escape. Kate becomes an outlaw who kills the sheriff and many others, leaving a lipstick kiss on each victim. It is told briefly, but it is a racist murder, and it is the moral heart of the book. At the camp, Zero hits Stanley in the face with a shovel, yellow-spotted lizards can kill with one bite, and the Warden scratches a counsellor's face with rattlesnake-venom nail polish. Language is mild. The 2003 film is rated PG.",
    publisherNote: "Farrar, Straus and Giroux publishes it as middle grade. It won the 1999 Newbery Medal and the National Book Award.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone. Small Steps, a companion about Armpit after Camp Green Lake, is written for slightly older readers.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, the Newbery and National Book Award records, the film's MPA rating as context and consistent teacher and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Louis Sachar: official site", url: "https://www.louissachar.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A kiss in the backstory, and its consequences."], [2, "A man shot in the backstory, an outlaw's killings, a shovel to the face."], [1, "Mild insults."], [1, "A counsellor chews sunflower seeds because he quit smoking."], [2, "A racist murder, a juvenile prison, homelessness, a wrongful conviction."]] }),

  u({ slug: "a-wrinkle-in-time", title: "A Wrinkle in Time", author: "Madeleine L'Engle", year: 1962, series: { name: "Time Quintet", slug: "time-quintet", index: 1 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Farrar, Straus and Giroux", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "Meg Murry crosses the universe to find her father. I stamp it ten-plus: nothing on the surface is worrying, but a planet where everyone bounces balls in rhythm and a giant brain that takes over a five-year-old are genuinely eerie, and the ideas are big.",
    summary: "Meg Murry is in her early teens, stubborn and miserable at school; her scientist father has been missing for a year. On a dark and stormy night, her strange little brother Charles Wallace brings home a new acquaintance, Mrs Whatsit, who turns out to be one of three otherworldly beings who know where Mr Murry is. With Calvin O'Keefe, a popular boy from school who feels as out of place as Meg does, the children 'tesser', fold space and time, to a planet called Camazotz, where every child bounces a ball in the same rhythm and every door opens on cue. There, a vast disembodied brain called IT controls everyone. Charles Wallace is taken over, and Meg has to rescue him and her father with the one thing IT does not have.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up, and taught from fourth to sixth grade. A younger reader can follow the adventure but will miss much of the science and philosophy. The frightening scenes are psychological rather than violent: the dark Thing, a shadow over the universe; the identical children on Camazotz; and Charles Wallace, speaking in IT's voice, cold and cruel to his sister. Meg is nearly frozen solid passing through IT's darkness and is nursed back by a gentle, eyeless creature she calls Aunt Beast. Calvin kisses Meg once. There is no swearing or violence on the page. The book is on the American Library Association's lists of frequently challenged books, mainly for its religious references. The 2018 film is rated PG.",
    publisherNote: "Farrar, Straus and Giroux publishes it as middle grade. It won the 1963 Newbery Medal.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of the Time Quintet. A Wind in the Door and A Swiftly Tilting Planet stay in the same band, with heavier ideas. Many Waters, about the twins and Noah's flood, has more mature themes and sits at twelve-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, the Newbery record, American Library Association challenge data and consistent teacher and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Madeleine L'Engle: official site", url: "https://www.madeleinelengle.com/" }],
    challenged: true,
    challengedNote: "A Wrinkle in Time appears on the American Library Association's lists of most frequently challenged books, usually for religious objections to its mix of Christian and other references. My band reflects the content; the challenge record is listed for context, not as a verdict.",
    sig: [[1, "One kiss."], [1, "Peril: a frozen girl, a possessed brother, a dark Thing."], [0, "Clean."], [0, "None."], [2, "A missing father, conformity enforced by mind control, a brother taken over."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-outsiders", title: "The Outsiders", author: "S.E. Hinton", year: 1967, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Viking / Penguin", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "Greasers, Socs and 'stay gold'. I stamp it twelve-plus: there is no sex and only mild swearing, but a stabbing, two deaths, constant smoking and a teenager who wants to be shot by the police make it a book to discuss, which is why seventh-grade teachers love it.",
    summary: "Ponyboy Curtis is fourteen, likes books and sunsets, and is a greaser, one of the poor kids on Tulsa's East Side, raised by his older brothers Darry and Sodapop since their parents died. The greasers are at war with the Socs, the rich kids from the West Side, who drive Mustangs and beat up greasers for fun. One night, after Ponyboy and his friend Johnny talk to two Soc girls at the drive-in, a gang of drunk Socs catches them in a park and holds Ponyboy's head under the water of a fountain. Johnny stabs one of them, Bob, to death. With help from Dally, the toughest greaser, the two boys hide in an abandoned church in the country, until it catches fire with a group of schoolchildren inside.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up, and taught in seventh and eighth grade across the country; a mature eleven-year-old can read it with someone to talk to. Violence is the main reason for the band: Johnny was badly beaten by Socs before the book begins, he kills Bob to save Ponyboy, he is crushed by a falling beam in the church fire and dies in hospital, and the big rumble between the gangs is a bare-fisted fight. Dally, grieving, robs a store and points an unloaded gun at the police so they will shoot him, which they do; it is suicide by police, though the book does not use the word. Johnny's parents are abusive. Everyone smokes constantly, and the Socs drink. Language is mild. There is no romance beyond Ponyboy's talk with Cherry Valance. The Broadway musical won the 2024 Tony for Best Musical; the 1983 film is rated PG-13.",
    publisherNote: "Viking, now Penguin, publishes it as young adult; Hinton wrote it at fifteen and sixteen, and it is often called the first YA novel. Content sits at the young end of YA.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone. Hinton's That Was Then, This Is Now and Rumble Fish are darker, with drug use, and sit at fourteen-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, long-standing teacher consensus, American Library Association challenge data, the film's MPA rating as context and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "S.E. Hinton: official site", url: "https://www.sehinton.com/" }],
    challenged: true,
    challengedNote: "The Outsiders appears on the American Library Association's lists of frequently challenged books, most often for gang violence, underage smoking and drinking, and family dysfunction. My band reflects the content; the challenge record is listed for context, not as a verdict.",
    sig: [[0, "A conversation with Cherry at the drive-in; nothing more."], [3, "A fatal stabbing, an attempted drowning, a burning church, a rumble, a police shooting."], [1, "Mild."], [3, "Constant teen smoking; drunk Socs start the fatal fight."], [3, "Orphaned brothers, an abused boy, two deaths and a death by police."]] }),

  u({ slug: "heartstopper-volume-1", title: "Heartstopper: Volume 1", author: "Alice Oseman", year: 2019, series: { name: "Heartstopper", slug: "heartstopper", index: 1 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Graphix (Scholastic) / Hodder", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "Two boys meet in a form room and fall for each other. I stamp volume one twelve-plus: it is a gentle graphic novel with kisses and no sex, but it includes homophobic bullying and an older boy who forces a kiss on Charlie, and the later volumes climb.",
    summary: "Charlie Spring is in Year 10 at an English boys' grammar school. He was outed the year before and bullied for it, and he is in a secret, unhappy arrangement with a boy called Ben who will only kiss him when no one is looking. Nick Nelson is in Year 11, plays rugby, and is kind. When they are seated together in form, they become friends, and Nick asks Charlie to join the rugby team. Charlie falls for Nick and assumes it is hopeless. Nick, to his own surprise, starts to wonder about himself, and searches the internet late at night, quizzes and all. The volume ends at a party at a classmate's house, in a quiet room away from everyone else.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up for volume one, and one of the most-asked books on this site for eleven- to thirteen-year-olds. Here is what is on the page. Romance: hand-holding, crushes and kisses, drawn sweetly; nothing sexual. The thing to know is Ben: he pressures Charlie into secret kisses and, when Charlie tries to end it, grabs him and kisses him against his will until Nick arrives. It is short and clearly framed as wrong. Charlie was bullied for being gay, and there are homophobic comments from rugby players. Language is mild British swearing, occasionally stronger. Teenagers drink at a party. The Netflix series is rated TV-14. The drift note matters: volume four deals with Charlie's eating disorder and self-harm, and volume five with a couple deciding whether they are ready for sex, without showing it.",
    publisherNote: "Graphix (Scholastic) in the US and Hodder in the UK publish it as young adult, ages fourteen and up. I place volume one lower because its content is gentle; I follow the publisher from volume four.",
    seriesNote: "Volume one of five so far. Volumes two and three hold the same band. Volume four (eating disorder, self-harm) and volume five (a conversation about sex) move to fourteen-plus for me.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the series' TV rating as context, PEN America's index of school book bans and consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Alice Oseman: official site", url: "https://aliceoseman.com/" }],
    challenged: true,
    challengedNote: "Heartstopper appears on PEN America's index of books removed or challenged in US school libraries, most often for its LGBTQ+ content. My band reflects the content; the challenge record is listed for context, not as a verdict.",
    sig: [[1, "Hand-holding and kisses."], [1, "A forced kiss, stopped; rugby knocks."], [2, "Occasional British swearing."], [1, "Teens drinking at a party, briefly."], [2, "Homophobic bullying, being outed, a coercive older boy."]] }),

  u({ slug: "enders-game", title: "Ender's Game", author: "Orson Scott Card", year: 1985, series: { name: "Ender's Saga", slug: "enders-saga", index: 1 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Tor", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "Child soldiers trained in zero gravity to save the world. I stamp it twelve-plus: there is no romance, but a six-year-old kills two other children, and the adults' manipulation of a lonely boy is the point of the book.",
    summary: "Earth has survived two invasions by an insect-like alien race called the Formics, the 'buggers', and the military is breeding and testing children to find a commander for the next war. Andrew 'Ender' Wiggin, six years old, is a Third, a rare third child in a world that limits families to two. His brilliant older brother Peter is cruel and violent; his sister Valentine is the only person who loves him. Ender is taken to Battle School, a space station where children train in zero-gravity war games, and the adults running it deliberately isolate him, turn the other students against him and push him harder than anyone has been pushed. He becomes the best commander the school has ever seen. His final exams, in a simulator, are not what he has been told.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up, and a book I see recommended to ten-year-olds who love science fiction; that is young for it, not because anything is graphic, but because of what the violence means. Ender, at six, kicks a bully, Stilson, until he stops moving; later, at Battle School, he fights an older boy, Bonzo, in a shower room and kills him. In both cases Ender does not learn the boy is dead until much later, and the reader does. Peter tortures squirrels and threatens to kill his brother. Language includes occasional swearing and invented insults. There is no romance. The ending is an act of genocide that Ender did not know he was committing, and the book's real question is what adults have the right to do to a child. The 2013 film is rated PG-13.",
    publisherNote: "Tor publishes it as adult science fiction. It is widely taught in middle and high school. The Marine Corps has put it on its professional reading list.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of the Ender's Saga. Speaker for the Dead and the later books are slower, adult and philosophical; the Ender's Shadow books retell this story from Bean's point of view in the same band.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's MPA rating as context and consistent teacher and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Orson Scott Card: Hatrack River", url: "https://www.hatrack.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None."], [3, "Two children killed in fights by a child; war games; an alien genocide."], [2, "Occasional swearing and invented insults."], [0, "None."], [3, "Child soldiers, deliberate isolation, a violent sibling, genocide."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-fellowship-of-the-ring", title: "The Fellowship of the Ring", author: "J.R.R. Tolkien", year: 1954, series: { name: "The Lord of the Rings", slug: "the-lord-of-the-rings", index: 1 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "HarperCollins / Houghton Mifflin Harcourt", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "Frodo leaves the Shire with a ring that wants to go home. I stamp it twelve-plus: the content barely rises from The Hobbit, with no romance and no swearing, but the Black Riders are frightening, the battles are longer, and the pace and length are where most younger readers stop.",
    summary: "Sixty years after The Hobbit, Bilbo vanishes from his own birthday party and leaves his magic ring to his young cousin Frodo. Gandalf discovers that it is the One Ring of Sauron, the Dark Lord, who is searching for it. Frodo sets out from the Shire with his gardener Sam and his cousins Merry and Pippin, hunted by the Black Riders, the Ringwraiths. They meet the ranger Strider at the inn in Bree, and on the hill of Weathertop Frodo is stabbed with a cursed blade. At Rivendell, the elf-lord Elrond calls a council, and a Fellowship of nine sets out to destroy the Ring in the fire where it was made: four hobbits, Gandalf, Strider, who is Aragorn, heir of kings, the elf Legolas, the dwarf Gimli and Boromir of Gondor. The road takes them under the mountains through the mines of Moria.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up. Searches for 'Lord of the Rings book age rating' are often from parents of a child who loved The Hobbit, and the honest answer is that content is not the barrier: the book is long, starts slowly, and spends a lot of time on songs, history and walking. A strong reader of ten or eleven who wants it will manage; many readers start and stop. The frightening moments are the Black Riders, Frodo's poisoned wound, the Barrow-wight that nearly entombs the hobbits, the orc attack in Moria and the Balrog. Gandalf falls. Boromir's attempt to take the Ring from Frodo sets up his death in the next book. There is no romance beyond a glimpse of Arwen, no swearing, and the substances are pipe-weed and beer at the Prancing Pony. The Hunt for Gollum, a new film in this world, is due in December 2027.",
    publisherNote: "HarperCollins and Houghton Mifflin Harcourt publish it for adults and children alike. Content is milder than most adult fantasy; length and style are what make it adult.",
    seriesNote: "Volume one of three. The Two Towers and The Return of the King have more battle, including the Battle of Helm's Deep and Shelob, but no sharper content; I hold the trilogy at twelve-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, long-standing critical and teacher consensus, the films' MPA ratings as context and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "The Tolkien Estate", url: "https://www.tolkienestate.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "A glimpse of Arwen; nothing more."], [2, "A cursed stabbing, orc battles, a Balrog. Not gory."], [0, "Clean."], [2, "Pipe-weed throughout; beer at the inn."], [2, "Temptation and corruption, the loss of a guide, a burden no one should carry."]] }),

  u({ slug: "lord-of-the-flies", title: "Lord of the Flies", author: "William Golding", year: 1954, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Faber & Faber / Penguin", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "Schoolboys stranded on an island build a society and then destroy it. I stamp it fourteen-plus: there is no sex and little swearing, but two boys are killed by the others, and the book's argument about what children are capable of is what makes it a high-school book.",
    summary: "During a war, a plane evacuating a group of British schoolboys crashes on a deserted tropical island. No adults survive. Ralph, fair-haired and decent, is elected chief and blows a conch shell to call meetings. Piggy, overweight, asthmatic and the cleverest of them, is his adviser. Jack, head of a choir, wants to hunt. The boys light a signal fire with Piggy's glasses and let it go out when a ship passes. The littluns talk about a beast. Jack's hunters paint their faces, kill a sow and leave its head on a stick as an offering, the Lord of the Flies. Simon, a quiet boy who goes alone into the forest, discovers the truth about the beast and runs to tell the others, who are dancing in a storm.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up, and taught in ninth and tenth grade; searches for twelve- and thirteen-year-olds are common, and a mature thirteen-year-old with a teacher or parent to talk to will manage. The violence is the band driver: a small boy disappears in a fire the boys start, the hunters kill a sow with a crude and disturbing scene of stabbing, Simon is beaten to death by the whole group in a frenzy, and Roger levers a boulder onto Piggy, who falls to his death on the rocks. The final chapter is a manhunt for Ralph. Language is mild, with a few crude words, and the book uses the language of its time about 'savages'. Piggy is mocked throughout. There are no girls and no romance. It is short, and the ideas are the reason to read it.",
    publisherNote: "Faber & Faber in the UK and Penguin in the US publish it as an adult literary classic. It is one of the most widely taught novels in English-speaking high schools.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, long-standing teacher and critical consensus, American Library Association challenge data and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Faber: Lord of the Flies", url: "https://www.faber.co.uk/" }],
    challenged: true,
    challengedNote: "Lord of the Flies appears on the American Library Association's lists of frequently challenged books, most often for violence and language. My band reflects the content; the challenge record is listed for context, not as a verdict.",
    sig: [[0, "None."], [3, "Two boys killed by the group, a brutal pig hunt, a manhunt."], [1, "Mild, with a few crude words."], [0, "None."], [3, "Murder by children, bullying, the collapse of order."]] }),

  u({ slug: "we-were-liars", title: "We Were Liars", author: "E. Lockhart", year: 2014, series: { name: "We Were Liars", slug: "we-were-liars", index: 1 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Delacorte Press", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "A rich family's private island, four cousins and a summer nobody will talk about. I stamp it fourteen-plus: no sex, mild language, but painkiller dependency, a twist built on the deaths of teenagers, and grief written as illness.",
    summary: "Cadence Sinclair Eastman is the eldest grandchild of a wealthy, old-money family that summers every year on its private island off Massachusetts. With her cousins Johnny and Mirren and Gat, the nephew of her aunt's partner, she forms a group the family calls the Liars. In the summer she was fifteen, something happened. Cadence was found in the water with a head injury, and she cannot remember any of it. Two years later she returns to the island, where she suffers crippling migraines, the adults are strange and fragile, and the Liars are glad to see her but will not explain. She and Gat pick up where they left off. As her memory comes back in pieces, so does the truth about her family's fight over money, and about what the four of them did to stop it.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up. Many twelve- and thirteen-year-olds ask for it, and it is short and gripping; the twist is the issue. Without giving it away: the ending involves a fire and the deaths of teenagers, and it reframes the whole book as grief. Cadence takes prescription painkillers for her migraines heavily enough to be concerning, and the adults drink wine constantly. Romance is Cadence and Gat: kisses and longing, nothing more. Language is mild. The family's treatment of Gat, who is Indian American, is about racism and class, quietly. The 2025 Prime Video series is rated TV-14. For a thirteen-year-old who loved A Good Girl's Guide to Murder, it is a step sideways rather than up.",
    publisherNote: "Delacorte Press (Penguin Random House), young adult. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "Followed by a prequel, Family of Liars, about Cadence's mother's summer, which has more drinking and some drug use and sits at fourteen-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the series' TV rating as context and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "E. Lockhart: official site", url: "https://www.emilylockhart.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "Kisses and longing."], [2, "A fire with fatal consequences, revealed late."], [1, "Mild."], [3, "Heavy use of prescription painkillers; adults drink constantly."], [3, "Teen deaths, grief, family greed, racism and class."]] }),

  u({ slug: "wicked", title: "Wicked: The Life and Times of the Wicked Witch of the West", author: "Gregory Maguire", year: 1995, series: { name: "The Wicked Years", slug: "the-wicked-years", index: 1 }, ageBand: "16-18", grade: "high-school", publisher: "William Morrow (HarperCollins)", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "The novel the musical came from, and not a children's book. I stamp it sixteen-plus: there are sex scenes, a sex club with a disturbing assault, murder and political violence, and it is much darker and stranger than the films.",
    summary: "Elphaba is born green, with sharp teeth, to a minister's wife in Munchkinland. Her mother takes a flower-drug to make sure her next child is not green, and her sister Nessarose is born without arms. At Shiz University, Elphaba rooms with the shallow, lovely Galinda, later Glinda, and becomes devoted to Doctor Dillamond, a Goat professor researching the rights of Animals, the talking creatures Oz is stripping of their rights. He is murdered. In the Emerald City, Elphaba joins an underground resistance against the Wizard and has a secret affair with Fiyero, a married prince, which ends in his death. She retreats into years of exile in his family's castle, where a boy called Liir may be her son, and becomes the Wicked Witch of the West in time for a girl from Kansas to arrive.",
    ripeFor: "Sixteen and up from me, and the most-asked question here is from parents of ten- to thirteen-year-olds who loved the films. The answer is to keep the musical and wait on the novel. Sex: Elphaba and Fiyero's affair is on the page, not graphic, and Elphaba's mother's affair is described. In the Emerald City, the students visit the Philosophy Club, a sex show, where a young man is assaulted in a scene that is disturbing and implied rather than described; he never recovers. Violence: a professor's throat is cut, a prince is killed by the Wizard's secret police, and there is political murder and torture. Language is moderate. The book is dense, philosophical and long, which will lose most young readers before the content does. For a twelve-year-old Wicked fan, the musical's songs and the films are the right version for now.",
    publisherNote: "William Morrow (HarperCollins) publishes it as adult literary fiction. It was never marketed for children, and the musical and films soften it heavily.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of The Wicked Years. Son of a Witch, about Liir, and the later books stay in the same band, with violence and sex at similar levels.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader and parent reporting, including widely shared comparisons with the musical.",
    sources: [{ label: "Gregory Maguire: official site", url: "https://gregorymaguire.com/" }],
    sig: [[3, "An affair on the page, not graphic; a sex show."], [3, "A murdered professor, a beating death, political killings."], [2, "Moderate."], [2, "A mother's drug use during pregnancy; drinking."], [4, "Sexual assault implied, persecution of Animals, a disabled sister, exile."]] }),

  u({ slug: "in-a-holidaze", title: "In a Holidaze", author: "Christina Lauren", year: 2020, ageBand: "16-18", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Gallery Books (Simon & Schuster)", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "A Christmas time loop at a snowy cabin, with the boy she has loved for years. I stamp it sixteen-plus: it is one of the gentler Christina Lauren books, but there is open-door sex near the end and frequent swearing, and Netflix's film, out December 2, 2026, is why teens are searching for it.",
    summary: "Every Christmas since she was a child, Maelyn Jones has spent the holidays at a cabin in the Utah mountains with her family and her parents' oldest friends and their children. She is twenty-six, stuck in a job she does not like, and has been in love for years with Andrew Hollis, a family friend who she assumes sees her as a little sister. At the end of this year's trip, after kissing the wrong brother and learning that the cabin is to be sold, she asks the universe to show her what will make her happy. Then a car crash on the way to the airport sends her back to the start of the holiday, on the plane to Utah. She lives the week again, and again, and each time she tries something different, until she tries telling the truth.",
    ripeFor: "Sixteen and up from me. It is a Christmas romantic comedy, mostly sweet and funny, and the romance builds slowly. What lifts it out of a younger band is one open-door sex scene near the end, described on the page, and steady strong language, the f-word included, from adults talking like adults. Drinking is social and constant: eggnog, wine, shots at the cabin. The car crash that triggers each loop is peril rather than gore, and nobody is hurt in a lasting way. There is nothing dark in the themes. Netflix's film, starring Maddie Ziegler, premieres December 2, 2026, and will likely be gentler than the book. For a fourteen-year-old who wants a Christmas romance, Let It Snow or Dash & Lily's Book of Dares are the closer fit.",
    publisherNote: "Gallery Books (Simon & Schuster) publishes it as adult romance. It is one of the lighter books in the authors' list, which is why it is sometimes shelved near teen titles.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's release announcement and consistent reader reporting, including widely shared heat-level notes.",
    sources: [{ label: "Christina Lauren: official site", url: "https://christinalaurenbooks.com/" }, { label: "Netflix: In a Holidaze", url: "https://www.netflix.com/tudum" }],
    sig: [[3, "A slow build to one open-door sex scene near the end."], [1, "A recurring car crash; peril, not gore."], [3, "Frequent strong language."], [2, "Constant social drinking over the holidays."], [1, "A family cabin being sold; a stalled life."]] }),

  u({ slug: "icebreaker", title: "Icebreaker", author: "Hannah Grace", year: 2022, series: { name: "Maple Hills", slug: "maple-hills", index: 1 }, ageBand: "18-plus", grade: "adult-crossover", publisher: "Atria Books (Simon & Schuster)", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "A figure skater and a hockey captain forced to share a rink. I stamp it eighteen-plus without hesitation: it is a BookTok romance with frequent, explicit sex scenes, and it is one of the books most searched for by thirteen- and fourteen-year-olds.",
    summary: "Anastasia Allen has trained her whole life to make Team USA, and she and her skating partner Aaron train at Maple Hills, a California university. When the hockey team loses its own rink, the coaches force the two programmes to share ice time, and Anastasia has to put up with Nate Hawkins, the hockey captain, who is charming, persistent and the opposite of everything she has scheduled into her life. The two start as rivals, become friends, and begin a relationship that is mostly physical before it becomes more. Meanwhile, Anastasia's partner is controlling, her coach is harsh about her weight, and she has been managing her food and her anxiety alone for a long time.",
    ripeFor: "Eighteen and up. The searches from parents of thirteen- to sixteen-year-olds are the reason this book is on the site: Icebreaker is shelved in the romance section, sold in supermarkets and recommended on TikTok next to YA, but it is an adult romance with several long, explicit sex scenes, described in detail. Language is frequent and strong. College parties involve heavy drinking. The heavier material is handled with some care: disordered eating, a coach who comments on Anastasia's body, a controlling skating partner, and anxiety. For a fifteen-year-old who wants a sports romance, there are YA options with the door closed; this is not one of them.",
    publisherNote: "Originally self-published, now Atria Books (Simon & Schuster) as adult romance. There is no YA edition.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of the Maple Hills series. Wildfire and Daydream follow other couples from the same college at the same heat level. Drift is flat at eighteen-plus.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting, including widely shared content warnings.",
    sources: [{ label: "Simon & Schuster: Icebreaker", url: "https://www.simonandschuster.com/" }],
    sig: [[4, "Frequent, long, explicit sex scenes."], [1, "Sports injuries; no violence."], [3, "Frequent strong language."], [2, "Heavy drinking at college parties."], [2, "Disordered eating, a body-shaming coach, a controlling partner, anxiety."]] }),
];
